<item>
  <title>CartPole: From-Scratch Q-Learning vs. PPO</title>
  <link>https://raymondww.github.io/blog/posts/cartpole-rl/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<p>CartPole is the “hello world” of reinforcement learning: balance a pole on a moving cart by pushing left or right at every timestep. Episodes end when the pole tips past ~12° or the cart drifts too far off center, and a “solved” policy holds the pole up for the full 500-step cap. It’s simple enough to implement from scratch in an afternoon, which makes it a good place to actually feel the difference between a hand-rolled tabular method and a library-backed policy gradient method, instead of just reading about it.</p>
<p>I built three things: a random-action baseline, a Q-learning agent built entirely from scratch (no Stable Baselines3, no neural net), and a PPO agent using Stable Baselines3.</p>
<section id="baseline-acting-randomly" class="level2">
<h2 data-anchor-id="baseline-acting-randomly">Baseline: acting randomly</h2>
